<h2 id="h-design-philosophy-strength-simplicity-and-purpose">Design Philosophy: Built for Hunters, Not Spec Sheets</h2>
<p>The Bear Alaskan XT does not try to win a speed contest. It aims to be the bow you reach for every season because it simply works. That means a length that balances well, a brace height that hides small form mistakes and a weight you will not resent on a long walk in.</p>
<p>The name hints at the target customer. Alaska means long hikes, cold mornings, big animals and shots that might come after hours of sitting still. A bow for those conditions has to be dependable first and flashy second. The Alaskan XT follows that thinking: a straightforward cam, a sensible frame length and an accessory package that gets you hunting quickly.</p>

<h2 id="h-ready-to-hunt-configuration-practical-convenience">The Alaskan XT RTH Package: What &ldquo;Ready To Hunt&rdquo; Means</h2>
<p>RTH stands for Ready To Hunt. Bear RTH packages are typically fitted with a sight, an arrow rest, a quiver, a stabilizer, a peep sight and a D-loop. Instead of buying each piece separately and paying a shop to install it, you get a matched setup in one box.</p>
<?= callout('note', 'Confirm the accessories for your year', 'The exact RTH components can change between model years and retailers. Check the listing or ask your dealer which sight, rest and quiver ship with the 2024 or 2025 Bear Alaskan XT RTH you are buying.') ?>
<p>The package is a real time saver for new bowhunters, but it is also a smart choice for experienced shooters who want a backup bow or a second rig for a family member. You still need arrows, broadheads, a release and practice points. At Better Outdoors we recommend getting the bow tuned at a pro shop even when the accessories come pre-mounted, because peep height and rest timing depend on your draw length and anchor.</p>
<p>Plenty of hunters eventually upgrade one or two RTH parts. The most common swaps are a <?= amz('single pin adjustable bow sight', 'single-pin slider sight') ?> for longer western shots and a <?= amz('drop away arrow rest compound bow', 'drop-away arrow rest') ?> for extra clearance with fixed-blade broadheads.</p>

<h2 id="h-power-delivery-and-shootability">Bear Alaskan XT Performance: Speed, Draw Cycle and Let-Off</h2>
<p>A 335 fps IBO rating puts the Bear Alaskan XT in solid hunting-bow territory. IBO speeds are measured under a fixed standard (a 30-inch draw, 70-pound draw weight and a light 350-grain arrow), so your real arrow speed will be lower if you shoot a shorter draw, a lighter weight or a heavier hunting arrow. That is normal for every bow on the market.</p>
<h3 id="h-dhc-xr-cam-system">The DHC-XR Cam System</h3>
<p>The DHC-XR cam is the engine of the Alaskan XT. Bear designed it to deliver usable speed without a harsh, jerky draw. You can expect a draw cycle that builds weight steadily and then settles into the valley, where the 80% let-off reduces the holding weight to a manageable fraction of peak.</p>
<p>That 80% let-off is a deliberate choice. Some bows offer higher let-off, which feels easier to hold, but a slightly firmer back wall and more holding weight help many archers stay solid through the shot instead of creeping forward. For hunters who may hold at full draw while an animal steps clear, 80% is a comfortable middle ground.</p>
<h3 id="h-draw-weight-choice">Choosing 45&ndash;60 lb or 55&ndash;70 lb</h3>
<p>Pick the range based on what you can draw smoothly while seated, cold and tired, not what you can muscle back in a warm shop.</p>
