<h2 id="h-accuracy-and-forgiveness-in-real-conditions">Accuracy and Forgiveness in Real Conditions</h2>
<p>Hunting shots rarely look like range shots. You may be twisted in a treestand, kneeling behind brush or shooting on a steep angle with cold hands. Forgiveness is what keeps a small mistake from turning into a miss.</p>
<p>Three features help the Bear Alaskan XT here:</p>
<ul class="checks">
  <li><strong>6.25-inch brace height:</strong> the arrow spends less time on the string, so minor torque has less effect.</li>
  <li><strong>33-inch axle-to-axle:</strong> a longer frame tends to hold steadier on target than very short bows.</li>
  <li><strong>80% let-off:</strong> enough relief to hold through a long wait, with a firm feel at full draw.</li>
</ul>
<p>Forgiveness does not replace practice, though. Shoot from your hunting positions, at realistic distances and in your hunting clothes. A <?= amz('bag archery target broadhead', 'quality broadhead target') ?> lets you confirm point-of-impact with the heads you will actually carry.</p>

